      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), we start by noting that such a number can be expressed in the form \\( 22k + 15 \\), where \\( k \\) is an integer. We need to determine the largest value of \\( k \\) such that the number is a 4-digit integer.\n\nThe largest 4-digit integer is \\( 9999 \\). Therefore, we set up the inequality:\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\leq 9999. \\]\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we first isolate \\( k \\) by subtracting 15 from both sides:\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984. \\]\nNext, we divide both sides by 22:\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22}. \\]\nCalculating the division, we get:\n\\[ k \\leq 453.818. \\]\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the greatest integer less than or equal to 453.818, which is 453. Therefore, the largest value of \\( k \\) is 453.\n\nTo find the corresponding largest 4-digit integer, we substitute \\( k = 453 \\) back into the expression \\( 22k + 15 \\):\n\\[ 22 \\cdot 453 + 15 = 9966 + 15 = 9981. \\]\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is \\(\\boxed{9981}\\).",
