      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to identify the largest 4-digit number and then adjust it to meet the congruence condition.\n\nFirst, let's identify the largest 4-digit integer. The largest 4-digit number is 9999.\n\nWe need to find the largest number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ x \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\]\nThis means that \\( x \\) can be expressed in the form:\n\\[ x = 22k + 15 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need to find the largest \\( k \\) such that \\( x \\) is still a 4-digit number. Therefore, we need:\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\nFirst, solve the lower bound:\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\]\n\\[ 985 \\leq 22k \\]\n\\[ k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\geq 44.7727 \\]\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.7727, which is 45.\n\nNow, solve the upper bound:\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984 \\]\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\leq 453.8182 \\]\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the largest integer less than or equal to 453.8182, which is 453.\n\nThus, the largest integer \\( k \\) is 453. Substituting \\( k = 453 \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15 \\]\n\\[ x = 9966 + 15 \\]\n\\[ x = 9981 \\]\n\nTherefore, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9981}\n\\]",
